<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <!--
            allowBackup is false, and deliberately.

            Android's automatic backup copies an application's private storage -
            here, the OPFS directory holding the entire SQLite database - to the
            user's Google Drive. For an application whose stated promise is that
            data never leaves the device, leaving that on would make the promise
            false, quietly, by default, on the first idle overnight charge.

            The cost is that a new phone does not inherit the data. That cost is
            already covered: Settings has an explicit backup export that writes
            a file the user chooses to keep, and an import that reads it back.
            A backup you performed is worth more than one you assumed.
        -->

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!--
        Permissions requested from the system: none.

        Capacitor's template requests android.permission.INTERNET. This
        application makes no network requests - the build is audited for that on
        every deploy, and the page's Content-Security-Policy is default-src
        'self' - and every asset it needs is inside the APK. Deleting the
        permission costs nothing and turns "it works offline" from a claim in a
        README into something anyone can check in Settings, App info,
        Permissions, without taking my word for it.

        Voice control does not change this. Speech arrives through the system's
        own recognizer (ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH, see SpeechPlugin), which holds
        the microphone itself, so this application needs no RECORD_AUDIO.
        Do not add it: the feature works without it, and the check below fails
        on it.

        The merged manifest does carry one entry, added by androidx:
        DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION, namespaced under this
        application's own id. It is a permission this application defines to
        keep other applications away from its broadcast receivers, not one it
        asks anybody for. The workflow allows that name and fails on any other.

        The WebView still loads: Capacitor serves the application from
        https://localhost through WebViewAssetLoader, which reads the APK's
        assets and never opens a socket.
    -->

    <!--
        Not a permission, and the only reason it is here.

        From Android 11 an application sees no other application it has not
        named, so SpeechPlugin.availability - which asks the package manager
        whether anything answers ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH - would report an empty
        list on every modern phone and the microphone button would never appear.
        Naming the intent restores the answer for that one query.

        This grants nothing and asks the system for nothing. It is not
        QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES, which is a permission and is not here; it makes
        visible only the applications that themselves declare they can do speech
        recognition. The check below still finds no permission at all.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.RecognitionService" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.action.RECOGNIZE_SPEECH" />
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
